<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Hunyadi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1406–<a href="/years/1456">1456</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Hunyadi János, Iancu de Hunedoara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Hunyadi was a Hungarian general and nobleman of Wallachian origin who led the fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. He was voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> from 1441, ban of Severin and regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1446 to 1453. His victory at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1456 halted the Ottoman advance for decades, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> became king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi.jpg" alt="John Hunyadi" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: János Thuróczy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Hunyadi_(Chronica_Hungarorum).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The first reference to John Hunyadi is a royal charter of 18 October 1409, in which King Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> granted Hunyad Castle and its lands to his father, Voyk, and four kinsmen including John. Voyk then served in the royal household as a court knight. Two 15th-century chroniclers, Johannes de Thurocz and Antonio Bonfini, say Voyk had moved from Wallachia at Sigismund's initiative, and several modern scholars accept this, while Ioan-Aurel Pop holds that he was a native of the region around Hunyad Castle. Hunyadi spent his youth serving at the courts of powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, but 15th-century authors give contradictory accounts of which ones. In 1432 he married Erzsébet Szilágyi; their sons were Ladislaus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1439 King Albert appointed Hunyadi and his brother bans of Severin after Hunyadi had raided Ottoman besiegers during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a>. In 1441 he became voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and captain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, taking charge of the southern frontier. He favoured an offensive strategy, using well-equipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and Hussite wagon forts and mobilising peasants alongside professional soldiers. In 1442 he defeated Mezid Bey at the Battle of the Iron Gate and a large army under Şehabeddin at the Battle of the Ialomița, the first time a European army had beaten so large an Ottoman force.</p><p>These successes led to the Long Campaign of 1443–1444, in which Hunyadi took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevac">Kruševac</a>, Niš and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> before cold and lack of supplies stopped the army at Zlatitsa. His later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> ended in defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> in 1444 and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-kosovo">Second Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1448. In politics he supported Vladislaus I in the civil war over the Hungarian throne. In 1446 the Diet elected him sole regent, with the title of governor, for the minor Ladislaus V. He held it until 1452, when the king gave him the title of perpetual count of Beszterce, the first hereditary title in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. At his death he held land of about four million cadastral acres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1456 Hunyadi defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> against an army led by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, a victory that halted Ottoman expansion towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> for decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii">Pope Callixtus III</a> ordered church bells rung at noon to call the faithful to prayer for the defenders, and the custom of the noon bell is traditionally linked to this. Hunyadi died on 11 August 1456. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> was elected king on 20 January 1458, the first time a nobleman without dynastic ancestry took the Hungarian throne. Hunyadi is regarded as a Hungarian national hero, and Romanian historiography also claims him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Hunyadi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>John Hunyadi</b>, voivode of Transylvania and later governor of the kingdom, won victories in the Long Campaign of 1443–1444 and lost the Second Battle of Kosovo in 1448, but in July 1456 broke the Ottoman siege of Belgrade with the preacher John of Capistrano.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After King Ladislaus the Posthumous died unexpectedly, Michael Szilágyi persuaded the Estates to proclaim his nephew Matthias, son of <b>John Hunyadi</b>, king.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The siege lasted until 22 July, and <b>John Hunyadi</b> relieved the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Hungarian-led crusader army under the regent <b>John Hunyadi</b>, seeking to avenge the defeat at Varna, met the Ottoman army of Sultan Murad II on the Kosovo plain.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-17">17 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ottoman army of Sultan Murad II defeated the Hungarian and Wallachian army of <b>John Hunyadi</b> on the Kosovo plain, ending his hope of reversing the defeat at Varna.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-20">20 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 15th century Hungary held it against the Ottomans, and <b>John Hunyadi</b> was ban.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-severin">Banate of Severin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemun">Zemun</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.85,20.4&amp;year=1438" aria-label="Zemun on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5036 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of John Hunyadi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5486 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5486 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.85,20.4&amp;year=1438">What was here in 1438? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
